Thankyou Petri! I don't mind at all, please feel free to post
any of my further thoughts on the Rincewind subject. When I sent
you the mail in the first place I'd only just thought about it,
now after a little more consideration I reckon the best way
to go about it is:-

The spellbook is a little like a scroll of punishment - but has
a big advantage (same idea as wielding the iron ball). Once
identified or read should appear in your pack as

'the Octavo (cursed)'.

When it has been read the book should NOT disappear, but stay
with you (the remaining seven spells in the book are very
concerned about the welfare of the 8th - the one stuck in
Rincewinds head)

I now think the reading should just take a single move, not the
80 or so I thought in the first place, 80 moves takes just too
long and to follow the Discworld storyline, the spell 'jumped'
into RW's brain.

The book itself should be very heavy (the weight of eight
spellbooks) and is undroppable until you've dipped it (or
prayed) or any of the other standard curse removers. Once
uncursed, the book should be droppable, and should teleport back
into your pack at random intervals whilst not in hell.

When you are low on hit points, just before dying the Octavo
should get you out of trouble (possibly teleporting you to
another part of the dungeon), this of course is the one
advantage of the spell. If you try to read the Octavo (or
any other spellbook) again the runes should be just too
complicated for you to take in.

After >2000 moves if you should happen to read a remove curse
scroll whilst confused you should be able to winkle the spell
out and regain your original stats/spells etc.

The book would not follow you to hell (too frightened of
burning ;-)), or get you out of trouble there - basically, you're
on your own.

I believe this gives sufficient advantages/disadvantages to the
RW character - comments?

Regards, John B.



